<p class="heritage-longread__dek">A standalone family history of the Lakeman line &mdash; from the reclaimed polder soil of seventeenth-century Noord-Holland, through the spice islands of the Dutch East Indies, to a Calgary house across the street from a man-made lake, and on to three children in whom the line meets four other families.</p><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-to-come-through"><span class="heritage-longread__eyebrow">Prologue</span>To come through</h2><p class="heritage-longread__kicker">The Netherlands → the Indies → Calgary · c. 1660 to today</p><p>Every long family has a word for what carried it through. The Dutch have one. They say <em>doorkomen</em> &mdash; to come through. It is sharper than <em>survive</em>, which is passive, and than <em>endure</em>, which is merely patient. It means to break out the other side of the gale and reach the next harbour: to still be there, working, when the cholera year ends; still raising children when the empire collapses; still there at all, when by every reasonable accounting you should not be.</p><aside class="heritage-line__quote heritage-longread__pq" aria-hidden="true"><p>The Lakemans were born onto land that had been made.</p></aside><p>The Lakemans practised <em>doorkomen</em> for four hundred years. They practised it in the reclaimed soil of the Beemster polder, patching dikes and burying children. They practised it through the long decline of the Dutch Republic, the Napoleonic occupation, the cholera epidemics of the 1840s and 1850s. They practised it when a seven-year-old orphan named Pieter was placed with kin in Purmerend in 1852 because the same disease that had taken his mother three years earlier had now taken his father. They practised it when that boy grew up to board a steamer for the Dutch East Indies and built a life eight thousand miles from his village; when his son rose to colonial mayor and then sailed his family home as the empire began to end; when his grandson Rienk took a geologist&rsquo;s doctorate and carried it around the world &mdash; Venezuela, Canada, England, Kuwait, Singapore &mdash; before settling, at last, in Calgary, across the street from a small lake dug out of farmland.</p><p>The name itself carries a hint of all this. Its deepest probable origin is not &mdash; as later generations assumed &mdash; <em>man of the lake</em>, but <em>Lakenman</em>: cloth-man, a maker or finisher of <em>laken</em>, woven wool. Somewhere around 1600, in the Waterland villages north of Amsterdam, a Lakenman family worked cloth for a living. By the time the records become legible in the mid-1600s they had moved onto the new polder soil of the Beemster &mdash; a reclaimed lakebed drained by forty-three windmills between 1607 and 1612 &mdash; and were learning to farm instead. The Dutch say, with characteristic dryness, <em>God schiep de aarde, maar de Hollanders maakten het zelf</em> &mdash; God created the earth, but the Dutch made themselves. The Lakemans were born onto land that had been made.</p><p>This is their story, one generation at a time, from a Dutch polder in the 1660s to an Alberta household in which the Lakeman line meets four other family streams and passes, together, to three children.</p><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-the-earliest-lakenmen-c-1580-1700-cloth-water-an"><span class="heritage-longread__eyebrow">Chapter One</span>The earliest Lakenmen (c. 1580&ndash;1700) &mdash; cloth, water, and the new soil</h2><p class="heritage-longread__kicker">c. 1580&ndash;1700 · the Waterland villages &amp; the new Beemster polder</p><p>The earliest figures in the Lakeman story exist as silhouettes &mdash; names that surface in fragmentary parish records, notarial rolls, and occupational lists, their biographies reconstructed from the patterns around them. A caution belongs here at the outset: the line becomes <em>proven</em> by primary record only from the 1840s forward (see the Notes). These earliest generations &mdash; back through the polder farmers to a 1673 Beemster baptism &mdash; are <em>probable</em>, resting on strong continuity of name and place rather than on a document for every link. What survives is enough to know the shape of the family, not yet each of the men.</p><figure class="heritage-line__figure heritage-longread__figure--right"><img src="<?php echo esc_url( home_url( '/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/Broek-Waterland-canal-view.jpg' ) ); ?>" alt="A canal and timber houses in Broek in Waterland, north of Amsterdam" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Waterland country north of Amsterdam, where a cloth-working family called Lakenman lived before the Beemster was drained. (Period scene.)</figcaption></figure><figure class="heritage-line__figure heritage-longread__figure--left"><img src="<?php echo esc_url( home_url( '/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/sijmon-1.jpg' ) ); ?>" alt="A 1663 Dutch notarial deposition in secretary hand" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A 1663 Waterland notarial deposition &mdash; witnesses giving evidence over a disputed dyke. Not a Lakeman document, but the very kind of record, and world, the earliest Lakenmen lived inside.</figcaption></figure><aside class="heritage-line__quote heritage-longread__pq" aria-hidden="true"><p>Names that survive only as smudges in baptism books.</p></aside><p>The deepest probable ancestor is <strong>Jan Pietersz de Lakenman</strong>, a cloth merchant or dyer working in the late sixteenth century around Edam and the Purmer &mdash; small Waterland towns north of Amsterdam, in a landscape about to be utterly transformed. In 1607 an Amsterdam consortium began the most ambitious land-reclamation project then attempted: the draining of the Beemster, an inland lake of 7,200 hectares. Forty-three windmills in three tiers pumped it dry over five years; by 1612 a geometric grid of fields, dikes, and tree-lined roads was laid across soil that had never before been stood on.</p><p>By mid-century, men named <em>Lakenman</em> appear in the new polder&rsquo;s records. <strong>Pieter Jansz de Lakenman</strong>, plausibly Jan&rsquo;s son, is named in early Beemster fragments. A <strong>Sijmon Pietersz de Lakenman</strong> appears in mid-1600s Edam notarial documents as a <em>lakenwerker</em> &mdash; a cloth finisher &mdash; preserving the family trade even as the family took up farming. And by 1673 a <strong>Sijmon Lakenman</strong> is recorded in the Beemster Reformed Church register as father of a daughter, Trijntje, baptized on 4 March: <em>&ldquo;Den 4 dito, is het kind van Sijmon Lakenman gedoopt, genaemt Trijntje.&rdquo;</em> It is the first parish record to anchor the family to Beemster in nearly the form the surname carries today.</p><p>This was the world of the Dutch Golden Age &mdash; Vermeer and Rembrandt at work nearby, the VOC the most valuable corporation on Earth &mdash; but the family took no direct part in that wealth. They were polder people. They paid their tithes to the Reformed church, took their turns at <em>schouwdagen</em> &mdash; the unpaid days of dike maintenance every polder farmer owed the collective &mdash; and raised children in thatched farmhouses built on raised mounds to outlast sudden floods. Their reward, generation by generation, was the soil itself: rich, black, and theirs to work as long as they could pay the rent and patch the dike. Everything that comes later &mdash; the colonial career in Java, the geologist in Calgary &mdash; was built by men whose names survive only as smudges in baptism books. They had a foothold on land that had been a lake within their fathers&rsquo; lifetimes. That was enough.</p><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-sijmon-sijmonsz-lakeman-c-1698-c-1755-steward-of"><span class="heritage-longread__eyebrow">Chapter Two</span>Sijmon Sijmonsz Lakeman (c. 1698&ndash;c. 1755) &mdash; steward of the polder&rsquo;s cycle</h2><p class="heritage-longread__kicker">c. 1698&ndash;c. 1755 · Beemster · the first solidly-traced Lakeman</p><p>The first Lakeman whose existence rests on a clear, datable parish entry is <strong>Sijmon Sijmonsz Lakeman</strong>, baptized in the Beemster Reformed Church around 1698. His father was almost certainly the elder Sijmon of the 1673 entry &mdash; making this Sijmon the man through whom the family&rsquo;s continuity into the modern era is first solidly traced.</p><figure class="heritage-line__figure heritage-longread__figure--right"><img src="<?php echo esc_url( home_url( '/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/KinderdijkWindmills-scaled.jpg' ) ); ?>" alt="A row of Dutch polder windmills along a canal" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The pumping windmills that kept the polder dry &mdash; the unspoken foundation of every Lakeman generation&rsquo;s livelihood. (Period scene.)</figcaption></figure><p>Around 1715 he married <strong>Grietje Gijsbertsdr Snijders</strong>, a woman of similarly deep Waterland roots, and they raised six children baptized in the Beemster registers between about 1712 and 1729 &mdash; among them a second son, <strong>Jan</strong>, born 1720, who would carry the line forward. The Beemster polder families of his day worked mixed dairy-and-grain farms &mdash; cattle, butter and cheese for the Purmerend markets, grain in the fields between the canals &mdash; and worshipped at the Reformed church whose register recorded their baptisms and burials; the Lakemans were part of that world, though the particulars of their own farm are not recorded.</p><p>His was the first generation around whom the family record is solid. He died around 1755, by which time his son Jan was a grown man with a farm of his own.</p><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-jan-sijmensz-lakeman-1720-1777-the-polder-farmer"><span class="heritage-longread__eyebrow">Chapter Three</span>Jan Sijmensz Lakeman (1720&ndash;1777) &mdash; the polder farmer and the Enlightenment beyond it</h2><p class="heritage-longread__kicker">1720&ndash;1777 · Beemster · the polder farmer the Enlightenment passed by</p><p><strong>Jan Sijmensz Lakeman</strong> was baptized in the Beemster Reformed Church on 3 November 1720, the second son of Sijmon Sijmonsz and Grietje Snijders. He lived his whole life within sight of the polder where he was baptized, and never had reason to leave.</p><p>In 1750 he married <strong>Guurtje Lourensdr Hoogetoorn</strong>, a Beemster woman born in October 1729 into another long-settled polder family &mdash; one known for steady service on the <em>heemraadschappen</em>, the boards that managed the dikes and canals. Together they raised six children recorded in the Beemster registers: Guurtje (1751), Willempje (1753), <strong>Zijmen &mdash; also called Sijmon</strong> (1756, who would carry the next link), Gijsbert (1764), Aagje (1769), and Cornelis (1775). Jan worked the same mixed dairy-and-grain farm his father had, in a polder economy that was, if anything, growing more sophisticated &mdash; better breeding lines, better plows, dikes rebuilt to higher standards after the 1717 Christmas Flood. The community ethic of <em>schouwdagen</em> remained the unspoken foundation of all of it: fail to patch the dike, and the sea took everything back.</p><p>Jan lived through the years of the Enlightenment and the first stirrings of revolution, but as far as the record shows his life stayed within the polder &mdash; the same farm, the same church, his whole life. He died in November 1777, aged fifty-seven, and was buried in the Beemster church grounds. His son Sijmon, twenty-one, took over the farm.</p><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-sijmon-jansz-lakeman-1756-1836-the-napoleonic-ye"><span class="heritage-longread__eyebrow">Chapter Four</span>Sijmon Jansz Lakeman (1756&ndash;1836) &mdash; the Napoleonic years and the new surname</h2><p class="heritage-longread__kicker">1756&ndash;1836 · Beemster → Beets · the Napoleonic years &amp; the new surname</p><p><strong>Sijmon Jansz Lakeman</strong>, baptized in Beemster on 2 January 1756, lived eighty years &mdash; and those eighty years contain the most disruptive transformation in Dutch history since the Reformation. The Sijmon born in 1756 inherited a Dutch Republic that had governed itself since the sixteenth century. The Sijmon who died in 1836 was a subject of a constitutional monarchy under King William I &mdash; a country reinvented, between those two states, four times over.</p><figure class="heritage-line__figure heritage-longread__figure--right"><img src="<?php echo esc_url( home_url( '/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/Beets-engraving-Sluyter-DJ.webp' ) ); ?>" alt="An engraving of the village and church of Beets, North Holland" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Beets, where Sijmon Jansz Lakeman was buried in 1836. (Period engraving.)</figcaption></figure><aside class="heritage-line__quote heritage-longread__pq" aria-hidden="true"><p>It was Napoleon, of all people, who pinned the surname permanently into place.</p></aside><p>The trouble began in 1795, when French revolutionary armies crossed the frozen rivers and met almost no resistance. The old Republic was abolished and replaced by the French-aligned Batavian Republic; the VOC collapsed under its debts in 1799; in 1806 Napoleon installed his brother Louis as King of Holland; in 1810 he annexed the Netherlands outright and began conscripting Dutch men into the <em>Grande Armée</em> &mdash; many to Russia, where most died. With Napoleon&rsquo;s retreat the Dutch declared themselves free in 1813, and the Congress of Vienna created the new Kingdom of the Netherlands in 1815. Sijmon, born a citizen of the old Republic, died a subject of a monarchy that had not existed in his youth.</p><p>One administrative shock is directly visible in the family record. In 1811, by Napoleonic decree, all Dutch citizens had to register fixed family surnames. Until then the Lakemans had used the patronymic system &mdash; <em>Jan Sijmensz</em>, Jan son of Sijmon &mdash; without a settled family name. After 1811 the family formally became <strong>Lakeman</strong> on the civil register. They had been called Lakeman or Lakenman informally for generations, but it was Napoleon, of all people, who pinned the surname permanently into place.</p><p>Before any of that storm arrived, in 1779, Sijmon had married <strong>Lijsbeth Klaasdr Kramer</strong> (baptized in Warder in 1758). They raised six children, all baptized in the Beemster Reformed Church: Jan (1780), Klaas (1781), <strong>Pieter Zijmonsz</strong> (baptized 13 April 1783, who would carry the family forward), Sijmen (1784), Louweris (1786), and Simon (1787). Lijsbeth died in 1826; Sijmon remarried in 1827 to Immetje Pronk, with no further children. Through all eighty years &mdash; Republic, client state, annexation, restored monarchy &mdash; his actual life remained that of a Beemster polder farmer. The polder was a place the gales of history blew over rather than through. He died on 8 September 1836 and was buried in the parish grounds at Beets.</p><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-pieter-sijmonsz-lakeman-1783-after-1850-the-last"><span class="heritage-longread__eyebrow">Chapter Five</span>Pieter Sijmonsz Lakeman (1783&ndash;after 1850) &mdash; the last steady generation</h2><p class="heritage-longread__kicker">1783&ndash;after 1850 · Beets / Purmerend · the last steady generation</p><p><strong>Pieter Sijmonsz Lakeman</strong> was baptized in the Reformed Church at Beets, just outside Beemster, on 23 April 1783 &mdash; one of the more solid pieces of primary documentation in the early Lakeman record. He was the third son of Sijmon Jansz and Lijsbeth Kramer.</p><p>Around 1805&ndash;1810 he married <strong>Mientje Adriaansdr Kruit</strong>, a Waterland woman of similar polder background, and they raised five children: Adriaan (c. 1806), Geertje (1808), <strong>Sijmon</strong> (c. 1819, the man whose cholera death would nearly end the line), Trijntje (c. 1822), and Claes (c. 1825). He held his modest acreage in Purmerend as a <em>pachter</em> &mdash; a tenant farmer &mdash; as most Lakemans had for generations, working the familiar cycle of spring sowing, summer haying, autumn harvest, winter dike-patrol.</p><p>What changed in his lifetime changed <em>around</em> the polder rather than on it. The Industrial Revolution reached the Netherlands in the 1820s and 1830s: steam pumps began replacing windmills, and the first Dutch railway &mdash; Amsterdam to Haarlem, 1839 &mdash; soon cut the journey from Pieter&rsquo;s butter and cheese to the city markets from days to hours. The old polder economy was, for the first time, being woven into a faster national network. Pieter lived through all of it without leaving home, dying sometime after 1850 at a substantial age. His third son, Sijmon, was the one to whom the family land and line passed &mdash; and Sijmon walked straight into the worst year the family would ever see.</p><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-sijmon-lakeman-1819-1852-the-year-the-line-nearl"><span class="heritage-longread__eyebrow">Chapter Six</span>Sijmon Lakeman (1819&ndash;1852) &mdash; the year the line nearly ended</h2><p class="heritage-longread__kicker">1819&ndash;1852 · Purmerend · the year the line nearly ended</p><p><strong>Sijmon Lakeman</strong> was born in 1819 in Purmerend to Pieter Sijmonsz and Mientje Kruit, the middle son, and inherited the working share of the family land. On 8 May 1842, aged twenty-three, he married <strong>Trijntje Tergand</strong>, then thirty-four &mdash; a woman of the Beemster country, born at Hobrede around 1808, some eleven years his senior. (The marriage record names his parents as Pieter Sijmonsz Lakeman, <em>landbouwer</em>, and Mientje Kruit, and gives Sijmon&rsquo;s own trade then as farming.)</p><figure class="heritage-line__figure heritage-longread__figure--right"><img src="<?php echo esc_url( home_url( '/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/trijntje.jpg' ) ); ?>" alt="An 1849 civil death register entry from Purmerend" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Purmerend civil death register, 5 February 1849 &mdash; the infant Cornelis Lakeman, &ldquo;kind van Sijmon Lakeman, arbeider, en Trijntje Tergand.&rdquo; One of the children lost in the cholera years. (Translated from the Dutch.)</figcaption></figure><figure class="heritage-line__figure heritage-longread__figure--left"><img src="<?php echo esc_url( home_url( '/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/WeeskamerGoes.jpg' ) ); ?>" alt="A Dutch Weeskamer (civil orphan-chamber) building" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A Dutch <em>Weeskamer</em> &mdash; the civil orphan board that registered the placements of seven-year-old Pieter and his surviving siblings in 1852. (Period scene.)</figcaption></figure><aside class="heritage-line__quote heritage-longread__pq" aria-hidden="true"><p>The whole line now hung on a seven-year-old boy.</p></aside><p>They had several children over the next seven years, and lost some of them in infancy as families of that time so often did. Two survived to carry on: a daughter, Mientje, and a son, <strong>Pieter</strong>, born in 1845 &mdash; who would become the most consequential figure in the entire Lakeman story.</p><p>Then, in 1849, the cholera came. It was one of the great pandemic waves of the century, travelling the new shipping and railway networks out of the Ganges Delta; Purmerend lost over a quarter of its young children in the 1849 wave alone. The cause &mdash; contaminated water &mdash; was not yet understood, and the standard treatments did nothing. <strong>Trijntje Tergand died on 20 February 1849</strong>, aged forty-one, in the midst of that epidemic; her newborn son Cornelis died days earlier. Sijmon remarried seven months later, on 16 September 1849, to <strong>Antje Root</strong>, a twenty-six-year-old Purmerend woman, and their daughter Marijtje was born in July 1850.</p><p>It did not last. In June 1852 the cholera returned, and on <strong>15 June 1852 Sijmon Lakeman died in Purmerend</strong>, aged thirty-three. The civil register is starkly bureaucratic: <em>Sijmon Lakeman, arbeider, overleden</em> &mdash; Sijmon Lakeman, labourer, deceased. The young children left behind &mdash; among them the boy Pieter, then seven &mdash; were placed with kin by the Weeskamer, the Dutch civil orphan board; the widow Antje remarried within a few years, scattering the household further. The whole line now ran through that one orphaned boy. His father was dead, his mother was dead, and brothers and sisters had died before him. He survived. The next chapter is the story of what he did with that.</p><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-pieter-lakeman-1845-c-1900-the-orphan-who-crosse"><span class="heritage-longread__eyebrow">Chapter Seven</span>Pieter Lakeman (1845&ndash;c. 1900) &mdash; the orphan who crossed the world</h2><p class="heritage-longread__kicker">1845&ndash;c. 1900 · Purmerend → Zeeland → the Dutch East Indies</p><p><strong>Pieter Lakeman</strong>, born in Purmerend in 1845, was seven years old when both parents and three of his four siblings were dead. The Weeskamer placed him with extended Lakeman or Tergand kin around Purmerend. The next two decades are thinly documented &mdash; but by his late twenties he had pulled himself out of an orphaned childhood into a working adult life.</p><figure class="heritage-line__figure heritage-longread__figure--right"><img src="<?php echo esc_url( home_url( '/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/bloemen-huizen-water-zierikzee-2-scaled.png' ) ); ?>" alt="Canal, flowers and houses in Zierikzee, Zeeland" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Zierikzee, the Zeeland port where Pieter Lakeman married Jacoba Leunis on 5 August 1874, before the couple sailed for the Indies. (Period scene.)</figcaption></figure><figure class="heritage-line__figure heritage-longread__figure--left"><img src="<?php echo esc_url( home_url( '/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/Map-of-the-Dutch-East-Indies-and-Gorontalos-commercial-shipping-network-1872-Source.png' ) ); ?>" alt="An 1872 map of the Dutch East Indies and its shipping network" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Dutch East Indies the family sailed into &mdash; their children born across the archipelago, from Muntok to Ternate to Java. (Period map, 1872.)</figcaption></figure><aside class="heritage-line__quote heritage-longread__pq" aria-hidden="true"><p>He left the polder, left the country, and raised his children on the other side of the world.</p></aside><p>On 5 August 1874, in the Zeeland port of Zierikzee, Pieter married <strong>Jacoba Leunis</strong>, born in the fishing village of Renesse on 1 February 1853 &mdash; daughter of Leendert Leunis and Katharina Willemina Panser. The marriage record gives Pieter&rsquo;s trade as <em>hulponderwijzer</em> &mdash; assistant schoolteacher &mdash; and names his parents as Sijmon Lakeman and Trijntje Tergand, the couple of the previous chapter: the documentary thread that ties the orphan of 1852 to the cholera household he came out of. Sometime in the late 1870s the couple made the largest move in the entire Lakeman record up to that point: they left for the Dutch East Indies.</p><p>He went as a teacher in the colonial school service. The records track his rise in it: <em>hulponderwijzer</em> &mdash; assistant teacher &mdash; at his marriage in 1874, and by 1881 <em>Oost-Indisch hoofdonderwijzer</em>, an East Indies head teacher, the rank named on his son&rsquo;s birth certificate while he was home on leave. The children&rsquo;s birthplaces map the family across the archipelago: <strong>Leonardus</strong> born around 1877 in Muntok, on the tin island of Bangka (himself a teacher in time); <strong>Catharina</strong> in March 1879; <strong>Geertruda</strong> around 1880; <strong>Pieter Karel Willem</strong> &mdash; the figure of the next chapter &mdash; born 27 January 1881 during a spell of home leave in The Hague; and <strong>Jacoba Helena</strong> in 1888.</p><p>What the family&rsquo;s daily life in the Indies was like is not recorded. By the late 1890s they had repatriated to the Netherlands for good. Pieter&rsquo;s death date is not yet confirmed; Jacoba outlived him by decades, dying in the occupied Netherlands in 1942. The orphan boy from Purmerend had done what none of his ancestors ever had: he left the polder, left the country, and raised his children on the other side of the world &mdash; and put a son on the path into the colonial administration.</p><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-pieter-karel-willem-lakeman-1881-1966-the-admini"><span class="heritage-longread__eyebrow">Chapter Eight</span>Pieter Karel Willem Lakeman (1881&ndash;1966) &mdash; the administrator and the long ending of the empire</h2><p class="heritage-longread__kicker">1881&ndash;1966 · The Hague → Java → the Netherlands</p><p><strong>Pieter Karel Willem Lakeman</strong> &mdash; known universally in the family as <em>P.K.W.</em> &mdash; was born in The Hague on 27 January 1881 during one of his parents&rsquo; returns to the Netherlands, and spent most of his childhood back in the Dutch East Indies, where his father was building his colonial career.</p><figure class="heritage-line__figure heritage-longread__figure--right"><img src="<?php echo esc_url( home_url( '/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/Magelang_Coat_of_Arms_1930.svg_.png' ) ); ?>" alt="The 1930 coat of arms of Magelang, central Java" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The arms of Magelang, one of the central-Java towns where P.K.W. Lakeman served as <em>burgemeester</em> &mdash; mayor &mdash; in the Dutch East Indies.</figcaption></figure><figure class="heritage-line__figure heritage-longread__figure--left"><img src="<?php echo esc_url( home_url( '/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/Dutch_colonial_architecture_in_Malang_Java_Indonesia.jpg' ) ); ?>" alt="Dutch colonial-era architecture in Malang, east Java" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Dutch colonial Malang, the east-Java city P.K.W. administered in the mid-1930s, as the empire he served began to end. (Period scene.)</figcaption></figure><aside class="heritage-line__quote heritage-longread__pq" aria-hidden="true"><p>His job was to patrol the whole district on horseback.</p></aside><p>He served in the <em>Binnenlands Bestuur</em>, the colonial civil administration, and rose to <em>burgemeester</em> &mdash; mayor &mdash; in the Dutch East Indies, in central and east Java. The post was not ceremonial: by his son&rsquo;s family account, his job was to <strong>patrol the whole district on horseback</strong>, not merely the town &mdash; real governance across a wide rural territory of rice paddies and volcanoes.</p><p>He married once, to <strong>Sophia Timmermans</strong>, who came from Franeker in Friesland &mdash; her father a police brigadier &mdash; and carried strong Frisian roots. They had <strong>two sons: Rienk</strong> (born 1 October 1918 in Soerabaja, who carried this line forward) and <strong>Pieter</strong>, who became a medical doctor and had two daughters, whom Martin met once as teenagers.</p><p>P.K.W. retired and sailed home to the Netherlands in <strong>1933</strong>, when Rienk was fifteen &mdash; the end of his colonial service, and as it turned out, near the end of the empire itself: the Japanese invaded in March 1942, defeated the colonial army in nine days, and interned every European in the colony; the Dutch fought a failed war from 1945 to 1949 to recover the islands, and Indonesia became independent in 1949. P.K.W. lived quietly in the Netherlands until his death in 1966, aged eighty-five. His grandson Martin remembers visiting him once, in his eighties.</p><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-rienk-lakeman-sr-1918-1993-the-geologist-who-cro"><span class="heritage-longread__eyebrow">Chapter Nine</span>Rienk Lakeman Sr. (1918&ndash;1993) &mdash; the geologist who crossed the world</h2><p class="heritage-longread__kicker">1918&ndash;1993 · Soerabaja → Venezuela → Calgary → London → Kuwait → Singapore</p><p><strong>Rienk Lakeman Sr.</strong> was born in Soerabaja, on the northeast coast of Java, on 1 October 1918 &mdash; son of P.K.W. and Sophia Timmermans. He grew up an administrator&rsquo;s son in the late years of the Dutch East Indies, and was fifteen when the family sailed home to the Netherlands in 1933. He earned a doctorate in geology, and that science would carry him around the world.</p><figure class="heritage-line__figure heritage-longread__figure--right"><img src="<?php echo esc_url( home_url( '/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/grandpa-lakeman-1.jpg' ) ); ?>" alt="Rienk Lakeman Sr. holding his infant grandson Thomas, Calgary, about 1980" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Rienk Lakeman Sr. &mdash; the geologist who crossed the world for the oil industry &mdash; holding his grandson Thomas, the keeper of these stories. Calgary, about 1980.</figcaption></figure><figure class="heritage-line__figure heritage-longread__figure--left"><img src="<?php echo esc_url( home_url( '/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/grand-marquis-lede-667dd777c385c.avif' ) ); ?>" alt="A 1970s Mercury Grand Marquis" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Mercury Grand Marquis that, by his grandsons&rsquo; accounts, &ldquo;drove like a ship.&rdquo; (Period model.)</figcaption></figure><aside class="heritage-line__quote heritage-longread__pq" aria-hidden="true"><p>The man who had crossed the world for a living ended up, again, beside water.</p></aside><p>He married <strong>Suzanna Verboom</strong>, born in Ter Aar, Zuid-Holland, on 25 September 1918 &mdash; five days before Rienk. The daughter of a tailor and barber, she had learned to sew and tailor from her father and became, by her son&rsquo;s account, a most excellent seamstress. She would live to one hundred, dying in Calgary in 2018.</p><p>Rienk&rsquo;s working life with the oil industry mapped the postwar world. In the early 1950s he worked for <strong>Royal Dutch Shell in Venezuela</strong>; when the government nationalized Shell&rsquo;s assets in 1955, the family &mdash; Suzanna, Rienk, and their two eldest sons &mdash; moved with a whole contingent of ex-Shell employees to <strong>Calgary</strong>, becoming Canadian citizens. In 1962 the company he worked for was bought by <strong>British Petroleum</strong>, and he took a post at BP&rsquo;s head office in <strong>London</strong>. From there the postings ran on: <strong>Kuwait</strong> in 1967, back to <strong>London</strong> in 1970, then <strong>Singapore</strong>. He retired in 1974, and on the way back to Calgary the family stopped in Holland and watched the spectacular 1975 New Year&rsquo;s fireworks before settling home for good.</p><p>He was a tall man &mdash; about six foot three &mdash; fluent in seven languages, devoted to classical music, the Olympics, world events, and the natural world. By the family&rsquo;s account he was still climbing trees at seventy. His Calgary house faced Lake Bonavista, a small artificial lake dug from farmland &mdash; so the man who had crossed the world for a living ended up, again, beside water. He drove a Mercury Grand Marquis the family called a &ldquo;land yacht&rdquo;; Suzanna drove a Camaro. He died in Calgary in 1993, at seventy-four.</p><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-suzanna-s-people-of-ter-aar"><span class="heritage-longread__eyebrow">Interlude &mdash; The Verbooms</span>Suzanna&rsquo;s people, of Ter Aar</h2><p class="heritage-longread__kicker">Ter Aar, Zuid-Holland · the tailor-barber&rsquo;s family</p><p>Rienk&rsquo;s wife brought a whole family of her own into the Lakeman story &mdash; a Zuid-Holland line as rooted in one village as the Lakemans had once been in the Beemster. <strong>Suzanna &ldquo;Suus&rdquo; Verboom</strong> was born in <strong>Ter Aar</strong>, among the canals and reclaimed fields south of Amsterdam, on <strong>25 September 1918</strong> &mdash; six days before Rienk himself. (The name is pronounced <em>Ver-bome</em>.) She lived to one hundred, dying in Calgary in 2018.</p><aside class="heritage-line__quote heritage-longread__pq" aria-hidden="true"><p>A needle, a trade, and a century of life &mdash; 1918 to 2018.</p></aside><p>What the family remembers most clearly is her father &mdash; Thomas&rsquo;s great-grandfather, the man Martin knew as &ldquo;Grandpa Verboom.&rdquo; He was a <strong>tailor and a barber</strong>, with the barber shop built right onto the house. He taught his daughter to sew and to tailor, and she became, by her son Martin&rsquo;s account, a most excellent seamstress &mdash; a skill she carried across the world and used all her life. Martin remembers staying at that house as a boy in the early 1960s, in a Holland still half a step out of an older century: the toilet just off the back door on a covered patio, a chamber-pail in the upstairs bedroom.</p><p>Suzanna was one of several Verboom children, and the family trade ran right through them. Her brother <strong>Pieter &mdash; &ldquo;Piet&rdquo;</strong> &mdash; and his wife Kelly kept a hair salon a block south of the family barber shop; Martin remembers the lot of them piling onto one oversized Vespa, Piet and Kelly and their two daughters all aboard. Another brother, <strong>Jacob &mdash; &ldquo;Yap&rdquo;</strong> &mdash; took over Grandpa&rsquo;s barber shop when the old man retired, so the shears stayed in the family and in the same town. And a sister, <strong>Jenny</strong>, with her husband <strong>Jan</strong>, kept the closest tie to the Canadian household: when the Lakemans came back from Singapore in 1974&ndash;75 they stayed with Jenny and Jan in Holland and watched the famous open-sky New Year&rsquo;s fireworks, and years later the couple crossed the Atlantic to visit Calgary &mdash; astonished, as Dutch visitors always were, at how much empty country Alberta held.</p><p>Beyond Suzanna&rsquo;s own generation the Verboom record goes genuinely dark. &ldquo;Verboom&rdquo; is a common name in the Ter Aar district, and an archive search turns up a well-documented local couple &mdash; a farmer, Cornelis Verboom, and his wife Aagje Donker &mdash; onto whom some online trees have grafted Suzanna. But that couple is not hers: their children are named in the records, and Suzanna is not among them; their trade was farming, not the tailoring and barbering Martin remembers. The match is a coincidence of surname and village, and this telling sets it aside rather than pass a wrong name down. Who Grandpa Verboom&rsquo;s own parents were, we do not yet know. (The Notes set out the records that would find them.)</p><p>After Suzanna, it went quiet. Seven thousand kilometres and a language barrier left the Calgary Lakemans all but cut off from the Dutch Verbooms; whether the barber shop and the hair salon are still run by Yap&rsquo;s or Piet&rsquo;s descendants, no one on this side of the ocean now knows. What survives, for certain, is what Suzanna carried with her: a needle, a trade, and a century of life that began in a canal village in 1918 and ended in a Calgary care home in 2018.</p><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-martin-gerard-lakeman-b-1957-the-calgary-generat"><span class="heritage-longread__eyebrow">Chapter Ten</span>Martin Gerard Lakeman (b. 1957) &mdash; the Calgary generation</h2><p class="heritage-longread__kicker">b. 1957 · Calgary · the first Lakeman born in Canada</p><p><strong>Martin Gerard Lakeman</strong> was born in Calgary on 30 March 1957 &mdash; the youngest of Rienk and Suzanna&rsquo;s three sons, and the only one born in Canada. His older brothers were <strong>Rienk Jr.</strong>, who became an engineer and worked abroad, and <strong>Alexander &ldquo;Lex.&rdquo;</strong></p><p>His was a borderless childhood. Because Rienk&rsquo;s career moved with the oil industry, Martin grew up across the postings: a preparatory school in England, where he spoke perfect Queen&rsquo;s English; an American school in Kuwait from 1967, after which, as he puts it, he &ldquo;spoke &lsquo;Merican&rdquo;; back to London in 1970; then Singapore. By the time the family settled in Calgary for good in the mid-1970s he had been schooled on three continents. He made his working life in Calgary as a taxi and limousine dispatcher.</p><p>In 1980 his son <strong>Thomas Martin Cheesman</strong> was born &mdash; the person through whom the Lakeman line runs forward into the present. The surname Thomas carries, <em>Cheesman</em>, came not from Martin but from his step-father Brian Cheesman&rsquo;s family &mdash; a chosen name, not a blood one. The Lakeman line is the blood; the Cheesman name is the channel it was poured into.</p><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-where-the-lakeman-river-joins-the-others"><span class="heritage-longread__eyebrow">The Living Line</span>Where the Lakeman river joins the others</h2><p class="heritage-longread__kicker">Calgary · Grande Prairie · where five rivers meet</p><p>Every long family story has a moment when it stops being history and becomes the present. For the Lakemans, that moment is now.</p><figure class="heritage-line__figure heritage-longread__figure--right"><img src="<?php echo esc_url( home_url( '/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/grandparents-with-fam.jpg' ) ); ?>" alt="Three generations of the family together under a tree" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The family today &mdash; three generations in whom the Lakeman line meets four others. Grande Prairie.</figcaption></figure><aside class="heritage-line__quote heritage-longread__pq" aria-hidden="true"><p>Doorkomen &mdash; to come through.</p></aside><p>The Lakeman line runs out of the Beemster polder, through the Indies and a world war and two oceans, and reaches the present through one person: <strong>Thomas Martin Cheesman</strong>, born in Calgary on 4 November 1980 &mdash; Lakeman through his father, Martin Gerard Lakeman. Thomas is the family&rsquo;s archivist &mdash; keeper of the slides and photographs, the family-tree spreadsheets, and the website where these stories live.</p><p>Calgary, the city he was born in, is where two sides of his own family had each arrived. The Lakeman line reached it when Rienk settled there after the war. His mother&rsquo;s people came from the other direction: the <strong>Docherty</strong> line out of Ireland and industrial Lanarkshire, and the <strong>McIver, Campbell, and Cameron</strong> lines out of the Hebrides by way of a Saskatchewan crofter colony &mdash; all carried down through his mother, <strong>Elizabeth Maryanne Cheesman</strong>, born Docherty.</p><p>Thomas and <strong>Melanie Lyn Haiste</strong> married in Calgary on 2 July 2016 and are the parents of three children. Melanie carries two more lines: the <strong>Haiste</strong> line through her father, Dan Steven Haiste, and the <strong>Rycroft</strong> and <strong>Steinke</strong> lines through her mother, Lana Lyn Rycroft. So five family lines now meet in one household &mdash; Lakeman, Docherty, and Hebridean through Thomas; Haiste and Rycroft through Melanie &mdash; gathered under the chosen Cheesman name.</p><p>The three who inherit it are a sibling-group, and they inherit jointly:</p><ul>
<li><strong>Patience Kristie-Anne Cheesman</strong>, born in Grande Prairie on 24 September 2013</li>
<li><strong>Daniel Eric Thomas Cheesman</strong>, born in Grande Prairie on 30 June 2015</li>
<li><strong>Faith Lana Lyn Cheesman</strong>, born in Grande Prairie on 30 March 2017</li>
</ul><p>None of them is <em>the heir</em>. Each carries the whole of it &mdash; the polder, the Indies, a survived world war, a crossing of two oceans, and, beside the Lakeman thread, the Irish, Hebridean, Yorkshire, and Peace-Country lines that met theirs.</p><p>There is one Dutch word the family kept for what carried it through all of that: <em>doorkomen</em> &mdash; to come through. Patience, Daniel, and Faith inherit it. The world they will live through is not yet written, but the family has come through before.</p><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-lineage-at-a-glance">Lineage at a glance</h2><div class="heritage-longread__table-wrap"><table class="heritage-longread__table">
