</table></div><p><strong>Confidence tiers:</strong> <em>Verified</em> &mdash; anchored to at least one primary register (DTB parish book, civil register, colonial service or government record). <em>Partly verified</em> &mdash; one anchor point against an otherwise inferred generation. <em>Inherited</em> &mdash; rests on family genealogy and circumstantial inference without primary verification yet. <em>Living memory</em> &mdash; recent enough to be remembered directly by family.</p><div class="heritage-longread__ribbon" aria-hidden="true"><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-node"><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-name">Sijmon Lakenman</span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-dates">fl. 1673</span></span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-node"><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-name">Sijmon Sijmonsz</span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-dates">c. 1698&ndash;1755</span></span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-node"><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-name">Jan Sijmensz</span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-dates">1720&ndash;1777</span></span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-node"><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-name">Sijmon Jansz</span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-dates">1756&ndash;1836</span></span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-node"><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-name">Pieter Sijmonsz</span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-dates">1783&ndash;after 1850</span></span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-node"><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-name">Sijmon</span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-dates">1819&ndash;1852</span></span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-node"><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-name">Pieter</span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-dates">1845&ndash;c. 1900</span></span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-node"><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-name">P.K.W.</span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-dates">1881&ndash;1966</span></span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-node"><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-name">Rienk Sr.</span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-dates">1918&ndash;1993</span></span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-node"><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-name">Martin</span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-dates">b. 1957</span></span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-node"><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-name">Thomas</span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-dates">b. 1980</span></span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-node"><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-name">Patience · Daniel · Faith</span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-dates">2013&ndash;2017</span></span></div><p class="heritage-longread__aside"><em>Side-branches named in the records but off the direct line: Pieter the emigrant&rsquo;s other children, born across the Indies &mdash; <strong>Leonardus</strong> (b. c. 1877, Muntok), <strong>Geertruda</strong> (b. c. 1880, Ternate), <strong>Catharina Wilhelmina Jacoba</strong> (b. 1879, Malang; twice-widowed, d. Hilversum 1960), and <strong>Jacoba Helena</strong> (b. 1888, Pasoeroean); P.K.W.&rsquo;s second son <strong>Pieter</strong> &mdash; a medical doctor in the Netherlands, with two daughters &mdash; alongside the direct line through Rienk; Rienk&rsquo;s other two sons, <strong>Rienk Jr.</strong> (an engineer who worked abroad) and <strong>Alexander &ldquo;Lex,&rdquo;</strong> alongside the direct line through Martin; and the deep polder households whose many children fill the Beemster DTB registers. The three children at the foot of the table inherit the Lakeman line jointly with the Docherty, McIver, Haiste, and Rycroft lines, gathered under the chosen Cheesman name.</em></p><details class="heritage-longread__notes">
<summary>Notes on the records</summary>
<div class="heritage-longread__notes-body">
<p>The Dutch keep good records. The <em>DTB</em> registers (<em>Doop-, Trouw- en Begraafboeken</em> &mdash; the baptism, marriage, and burial books of the Reformed Church), and the civil registration that Napoleon imposed in 1811, are among the best-preserved in Europe, and much of the Lakeman material is now digitised and indexed across the Dutch archival platforms. That is why the heart of this story can be told with real confidence &mdash; and why the parts that <em>aren&rsquo;t</em> yet proven can be named precisely.</p><h3>The verified spine</h3><p>The middle of this story is now anchored to primary civil records, and the join that matters most &mdash; the one connecting the nineteenth-century polder family to the colonial administrator&rsquo;s branch &mdash; is <strong>proven, not inferred</strong>:</p><ul>
<li><strong>P.K.W. Lakeman&rsquo;s birth</strong>, The Hague, 27 January 1881 (Haags Gemeentearchief, BS Geboorte, akte 368), names his parents as <strong>Pieter Lakeman</strong>, an <em>Oost-Indisch hoofdonderwijzer</em> (East Indies head teacher) on leave, and <strong>Jacoba Leunis</strong>.</li>
<li><strong>Pieter and Jacoba&rsquo;s marriage</strong>, Zierikzee, 5 August 1874 (Zeeuws Archief, akte 33), gives Pieter as born <strong>16 November 1845 in Purmerend, son of Sijmon Lakeman and Trijntje Tergand</strong> &mdash; the same cholera-struck couple of Chapter Six. So the line <em>Sijmon (d. 1852) → the orphan Pieter (b. 1845) → P.K.W. (b. 1881)</em> is sealed by two independent civil records.</li>
<li><strong>Sophia Timmermans</strong>, P.K.W.&rsquo;s wife, is confirmed by her 1955 Bennebroek death record as the daughter of <strong>Gerardus Johannes Timmermans</strong> (a police brigadier) and <strong>Riemke de Vries</strong>, of Franeker, Friesland.</li>
<li>Pieter&rsquo;s son <strong>Leonardus</strong>, born Muntok (Bangka) c. 1877, appears in his own 1903 Amsterdam marriage as an <em>onderwijzer</em> &mdash; confirming the family&rsquo;s presence in the Indies by the late 1870s and the teaching trade running through it.</li>
</ul><h3>The seam still open</h3><p>One genuine gap remains, and the story does not paper over it: <strong>the deep Beemster chain before 1845.</strong> The link from Sijmon (b. 1819) back through the eighteenth-century polder farmers to the 1673 Beemster baptism of Trijntje, daughter of Sijmon Lakenman, rests on strong name-and-place continuity and family-tree compilations, but each generational link has not yet been imaged from the DTB and civil registers. Treat Chapters One through Five as <em>probable, by onomastic and geographic continuity</em> &mdash; believed and consistent, but not yet sealed the way the post-1845 generations now are.</p><h3>The Verboom side &mdash; a false match set aside</h3><p>Suzanna Verboom&rsquo;s own generation is firm from family testimony, but the line above her father is not yet found, and one wrong turn has been ruled out. Several online trees attach Suzanna to a Ter Aar couple, <strong>Cornelis Verboom (a farmer) and Aagje Donker</strong>. An archive check disproves it: that couple&rsquo;s children are documented &mdash; Trijntje (1910), Catharina (1911), Cornelia (1912), Petronella (1913), an infant Hendrica (1915), Cornelis (1919) &mdash; and <strong>no Suzanna of September 1918 is among them</strong>; the father&rsquo;s trade was farming, not the tailoring-and-barbering the family remembers. It is a same-surname, same-village coincidence, and it is set aside here. Suzanna&rsquo;s actual parents remain unidentified in digitized records.</p><h3>Records that would close the remaining gaps</h3><ol>
<li><strong>Suzanna Verboom&rsquo;s own birth record</strong>, Ter Aar, September 1918 (Streekarchief Rijnlands Midden / Alphen aan den Rijn, or Nieuwkoop civil registration) &mdash; the decisive document: it names her true parents and her father&rsquo;s occupation. Confirm the exact date (25 or 28 September).</li>
<li><strong>The marriage record of Suzanna&rsquo;s parents</strong> (Ter Aar or a neighbouring municipality, c. 1910&ndash;1917) and the <strong>Ter Aar population register</strong> for ~1910&ndash;1930 &mdash; to name both sets of grandparents and enumerate the real siblings (Piet, Yap, Jenny, and the sister lost in infancy).</li>
<li><strong>Pieter Lakeman&rsquo;s own birth record</strong>, Purmerend, 16 Nov 1845 (Noord-Hollands Archief) &mdash; to confirm Sijmon and Trijntje as his parents from his <em>own</em> register, not only his marriage.</li>
<li><strong>The Beemster DTB and Purmerend civil registers</strong> for the 1673→1845 links (Noord-Hollands Archief) &mdash; to turn the deep polder chain from <em>probable</em> to <em>verified</em>.</li>
<li><strong>Colonial education / personnel files</strong> for Pieter and administrative files for P.K.W. (Nationaal Archief, The Hague; <em>Regeeringsalmanak voor Nederlandsch-Indië</em>) &mdash; for the exact postings, schools, and dates.</li>
</ol><p><strong>The Canadian generations</strong> &mdash; Rienk&rsquo;s postwar career onward &mdash; rest on Canadian records and living family memory.</p><p class="heritage-longread__aside"><em>In short: the spine is now proven by record from the cholera household forward; the polder generations above it remain probable, awaiting the parish books that would seal them.</em></p>
